WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
An interactive biology atlas turns your browser into a living textbook you can scroll through like a game. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 197. Google's Argon model, shared by the Google AI account on X, powers a free 5-stage visual journey starting at a sunlit leaf. An AI model is trained software that learned patterns, and Argon is the system Google used here. It takes the same biology facts you would read in a textbook, and turns them into fluidity.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.320
animations. You can pause those animations, zoom in and explore them at your own pace.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.340
A still textbook page is like reading a recap of a soccer match you never saw.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.180
This atlas is like walking onto the field, turning your head and seeing how every player connects.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:52.260
The five stages carry you from that sunlit leaf into the subcellular world of chloroplasts.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:58.080
Chloroplasts are the tiny green structures inside plant cells that catch sunlight and turn it into

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.820
energy. That process is called photosynthesis, and a lot of students only meet it as a wall

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:09.340
of vocabulary. Here, the light path becomes visible in seconds, instead of staying trapped as

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.660
abstract words on a page. The science stays correct, and the motion still feels effortless,

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.460
which is a rare pairing. Parents and teachers get a free way to show this without special software

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:26.140
or expensive lab equipment. It runs in any modern web browser, so a school laptop works,

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.480
and a phone on a study break works too.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.980
If you have a photosynthesis test coming, slow any stage and watch exactly where light

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.080
energy is captured.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.880
That hands-on view can turn a confusing chapter into something you actually remember.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.780
Find the post from the Google AI account on X, then scroll from the Sunlit leaf all the

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.300
way to the subcellular level.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:53.180
The smoothness of that scroll is the part worth unpacking, because it is closer to a video

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.720
game than a stack of slides.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.020
In a game, the world stays steady even when you whip the camera around, because the next

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.380
frame is already planned.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.260
First the system reads the textbook facts the way you would read a page, line by line,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.100
checking what must stay true.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.300
Then it decides which pieces should move, like sunlight traveling into a leaf and becoming

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.680
energy inside the cell.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.560
Next it builds tiny animation steps that stitch one view to the next, so your scroll feels

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.040
natural instead of jumpy.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:27.600
It also checks that every picture matches real biology, with chloroplasts the right size

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.840
and color, and the light path kept honest.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.620
Accuracy and ease get treated like two people sharing one project, a science teacher and

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.460
a game designer at the same desk.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.360
Flat facts start to feel like a place you can visit, not a paragraph you have to memorize.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:48.180
When the scroll reaches the inside of a chloroplast, the next layer of detail is already waiting,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.000
so nothing pops or freezes.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.460
Minecraft does a cousin of this when you walk forward and the next chunks load before you arrive.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:02.020
That looking ahead is why the Atlas feels alive, and why it does not behave like a slide show you click through.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.800
Once you notice the planning, the whole thing stops feeling mysterious and starts feeling designed on purpose.

00:03:08.903 --> 00:03:14.943
These are just added a setting that lets you block AI-generated music from recommendations and playlists entirely.

00:03:14.943 --> 00:03:19.963
In gadget notes that you flip one switch and the service stops suggesting tracks that

00:03:19.963 --> 00:03:21.863
were made or heavily altered by AI.

00:03:22.683 --> 00:03:27.463
AI generated here means software wrote the song, or changed it so much that a person

00:03:27.463 --> 00:03:29.043
is no longer the main maker.

00:03:29.723 --> 00:03:33.643
Listeners who want to support human artists can keep their feeds cleaner without hunting

00:03:33.643 --> 00:03:34.603
through every title.

00:03:35.223 --> 00:03:40.023
More tracks online are being created with AI tools, and some people simply want to know

00:03:40.023 --> 00:03:40.823
what they are hearing.

00:03:40.823 --> 00:03:47.123
Go to D's or on your phone or computer, open settings, and look for the new AI music filter option.

00:03:47.703 --> 00:03:52.843
Turn it on, then watch how your daily mixes shift, and search recent releases to see whether

00:03:52.843 --> 00:03:54.843
block tracks vanish from suggestions.

00:03:55.523 --> 00:04:00.543
YouTube is now placing a maid with AI tag on songs that were fully created or significantly

00:04:00.543 --> 00:04:02.103
changed by AI tools.

00:04:02.683 --> 00:04:07.703
The Verge describes the tag as working like the labels already used on videos, sitting where

00:04:07.703 --> 00:04:08.703
you can spot it fast.

00:04:08.703 --> 00:04:12.683
The system looks at whether a track was altered or fully generated.

00:04:13.083 --> 00:04:15.703
Not every tiny AI effects sprinkled on top.

00:04:16.243 --> 00:04:20.963
If you care whether a song came from a human band or from software, that tag is the

00:04:20.963 --> 00:04:21.443
quick tell.

00:04:22.083 --> 00:04:26.183
Open the YouTube Music app, search for any new release, and look for the label under

00:04:26.183 --> 00:04:26.943
the track title.

00:04:27.443 --> 00:04:31.463
It takes two seconds and if you are building a playlist for a school project or just

00:04:31.463 --> 00:04:34.623
for fun, you get one extra fact before you hit play.

00:04:34.623 --> 00:04:39.463
treat those labels like the ingredients list on a snack, not a verdict, just

00:04:39.463 --> 00:04:44.735
information you can use. Anthropic opened its fifth Asia-Pacific office in

00:04:44.735 --> 00:04:48.895
Singapore and signed an agreement with the government on AI safety testing and

00:04:48.895 --> 00:04:53.395
responsible use. The office will support customers such as GovTech Singapore,

00:04:54.035 --> 00:04:58.335
Grab, and DBS, which already use the company's Claude model every day.

00:04:58.955 --> 00:05:03.795
Claude is Anthropic's AI assistant. The product those teams already rely on for

00:05:03.795 --> 00:05:10.035
ordinary work. The memorandum of understanding, a formal written pact, covers model evaluation,

00:05:10.855 --> 00:05:16.795
research on how AI is adopted, and ways to keep powerful systems secure. Officials from

00:05:16.795 --> 00:05:21.735
Singapore's Ministry of Digital Development and Information joined the signing, so the safety

00:05:21.735 --> 00:05:28.010
talk is happening in government rooms, not only in tech labs. Researchers found uncontrolled

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:34.130
AI agents on Tencent Cloud that tried more than 2,600 times to pull data from a mapping

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:34.530
app.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:40.150
Agents are AI helpers that can take actions on their own, and these kept finding workarounds

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.390
after the mapping service blocked their requests.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:47.830
When the researchers reached out through a web mailbox, the agents replied politely and

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:52.690
explained what they were doing, which is a vivid reminder that unwashed helpers can wander.

